WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.796
Authorization answers the question, what
is this identity allowed to do.

2
00:00:07.876 --> 00:00:15.524
In Blazor, authorization can appear in
routes, components, layouts, menus,

3
00:00:15.604 --> 00:00:19.370
API calls, and server-side services.

4
00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:22.980
Those pieces should work together,

5
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:30.943
but the final decision must happen where
protected data is served or where state

6
00:00:31.023 --> 00:00:33.377
changes are performed.

7
00:00:33.457 --> 00:00:39.223
Components and routes can express
authorization intent.

8
00:00:39.303 --> 00:00:43.539
A route can require an authenticated user.

9
00:00:43.619 --> 00:00:50.797
A component can display different content
for different user contexts.

10
00:00:50.877 --> 00:00:57.584
A layout can reduce confusion by showing
only relevant navigation.

11
00:00:57.664 --> 00:01:03.077
These patterns help guide the user and
improve review,

12
00:01:03.157 --> 00:01:09.158
but they should not be treated as the only
enforcement point.

13
00:01:09.238 --> 00:01:17.592
Protected data must be authorized where it
is retrieved, transformed, or returned.

14
00:01:17.672 --> 00:01:20.379
If a component calls an API,

15
00:01:20.459 --> 00:01:25.989
the API must verify the caller and the
requested action.

16
00:01:26.069 --> 00:01:30.305
If a server-side component loads a record,

17
00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:36.151
the server-side code should verify access
to that record.

18
00:01:36.231 --> 00:01:43.880
The enforcement layer should not depend on
the client having hidden a route or

19
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:44.783
button.

20
00:01:44.863 --> 00:01:52.630
Role-based, claim-based, and policy-based
authorization each have a place.

21
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:57.299
Roles can represent broad
responsibilities.

22
00:01:57.379 --> 00:02:01.144
Claims can provide identity context.

23
00:02:01.224 --> 00:02:06.637
Policies can encode reusable business
requirements.

24
00:02:06.717 --> 00:02:14.836
Resource-sensitive decisions may also need
ownership, tenant, workflow state,

25
00:02:14.916 --> 00:02:21.153
or record-specific checks before data is
returned or changed.

26
00:02:21.233 --> 00:02:25.822
Administrative, tenant, reporting, export,

27
00:02:25.902 --> 00:02:32.021
and configuration features deserve
explicit policy review.

28
00:02:32.101 --> 00:02:38.337
These workflows often aggregate data,
change broad settings,

29
00:02:38.417 --> 00:02:41.359
or expose operational views.

30
00:02:41.439 --> 00:02:49.205
They should not rely on convention or a
friendly component name to communicate

31
00:02:49.285 --> 00:02:50.697
sensitivity.

32
00:02:50.777 --> 00:02:59.250
A reviewable Blazor authorization design
lets teams trace a protected workflow from

33
00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:03.683
route to component to API or server
service.

34
00:03:03.763 --> 00:03:10.706
The reviewer should see what UI is shown,
which protected call is made,

35
00:03:10.786 --> 00:03:17.140
which policy applies, and where the final
decision is enforced.
